WEBVTT


00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.148
A large language model is one mathematical function.

00:00:04.348 --> 00:00:10.347
It reads a sequence of tokens and returns a probability for every possible next token.

00:00:10.547 --> 00:00:15.408
That is the whole interface: text in, a distribution over the next token out.

00:00:15.608 --> 00:00:22.155
LLaMA-3-Lite is a compact, from-scratch model built in the LLaMA-3 style.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:30.914
Sixteen decoder blocks, a model dimension of one thousand twenty-four, and about five hundred fifteen million parameters.

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:34.931
The same model width runs through all sixteen blocks.

00:00:35.131 --> 00:00:38.641
Take the sentence on screen: The cat sat on the mat.

00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:43.929
Six tokens, six IDs, and one question: what comes next?

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:50.860
One token walks in as an ID and walks out as a probability over the whole vocabulary.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.643
Follow that walk from raw text to the sampling loop, one stage at a time.

00:00:55.843 --> 00:01:01.459
Each stage exists to fix a specific problem the previous stage leaves open.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:06.337
The loop at the end is what turns one prediction into a paragraph.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:11.075
Before any architecture, ask what the model is trained to do.

00:01:11.275 --> 00:01:18.196
The objective is next-token cross-entropy, plus a small z-loss at one ten-thousandth.

00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:22.969
Training shows the model real text with the next token hidden.

00:01:23.169 --> 00:01:26.410
The model assigns that hidden token a probability.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.658
When the probability is low, training adjusts the weights to raise it.

00:01:30.858 --> 00:01:36.970
That adjustment is the whole of learning: every weight moves to make real text more likely.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:44.336
Next-token training needs no extra manual labels: no sentiment tags, no answers, no rules.

00:01:44.536 --> 00:01:49.950
The loss measures surprise: how low the model's probability was for the true token.

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:55.060
Pushing surprise down is what shapes the five hundred fifteen million parameters.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.751
From that single objective, grammar, style and world knowledge settle into the weights.

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:03.721
Nothing in the code says what a noun is.

00:02:03.921 --> 00:02:08.157
Training runs that prediction at every position in parallel.

00:02:08.357 --> 00:02:11.773
Generation instead reveals one new token at a time.

00:02:11.973 --> 00:02:16.570
The structure you are about to see is the machinery that makes the prediction possible.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:20.040
Each piece is there because the objective demands it.

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:25.152
Text cannot enter a matrix multiply, so the tokenizer goes first.

00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:33.541
It splits text into subword pieces from a vocabulary of one hundred twenty-eight thousand two hundred fifty-six tokens.

00:02:33.741 --> 00:02:39.729
The scheme is byte-pair encoding: the most frequent character pairs merge into new tokens.

00:02:39.929 --> 00:02:45.105
Building it starts from single characters and keeps merging the most common pair.

00:02:45.305 --> 00:02:52.580
Common words stay whole, and rare words break into smaller pieces the model has seen before.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:59.706
Characters alone would make sequences far too long; whole words alone would explode the vocabulary.

00:02:59.906 --> 00:03:06.479
Subwords sit in between and cover any input, including words the model has never seen.

00:03:06.679 --> 00:03:09.697
Their IDs come straight from the vocabulary table.

00:03:09.897 --> 00:03:16.785
The word cat becomes eight thousand four hundred fifteen; the word mat becomes five thousand six hundred thirty-four.

00:03:16.985 --> 00:03:21.985
Each ID is just a row number; it carries no meaning of its own.

00:03:22.185 --> 00:03:25.087
The tokenizer knows nothing about cats.

00:03:25.287 --> 00:03:29.247
Its only job is to cut text into pieces the model has a row for.

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:35.729
The config lists one hundred twenty-eight thousand rows; the tokenizer holds two hundred fifty-six more.

00:03:35.929 --> 00:03:40.461
The embedding table sizes itself to the tokenizer, not to the config.

00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:46.159
An ID is only a row number; the row it points to carries the meaning.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:51.564
That lookup is the embedding matrix, one row per vocabulary item.

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:58.269
Each row holds one thousand twenty-four numbers, so every token becomes a vector of that length.

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:04.805
A lookup is all it takes: the ID picks the row, and the row becomes the token's vector.

00:04:05.005 --> 00:04:08.859
There is no computation here yet, only a learned table.

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:14.814
Training can move these rows so tokens used in similar ways land near each other.

00:04:15.014 --> 00:04:20.360
This is how it can learn semantic geometry: directions that carry meaning.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.888
An illustrative sketch might place cat near kitten and far from car.

00:04:25.088 --> 00:04:30.673
Because the table is learned, it embeds each token by how that token is used.

00:04:30.873 --> 00:04:36.540
The vector is a point in one thousand twenty-four dimensions, far too many to picture at once.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:40.619
Two dimensions stand in for the rest whenever the screen shows a sketch.

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:45.792
From this point on the model sees only vectors, never characters.

00:04:45.992 --> 00:04:50.178
From here, each token carries its vector through all sixteen blocks.

00:04:50.378 --> 00:04:54.090
That carried vector is called the residual stream.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:05:01.038
Inside a block, attention and SwiGLU each read a normalized copy of the stream.

00:05:01.238 --> 00:05:05.392
Each writes its result back onto the stream by simple addition.

00:05:05.592 --> 00:05:10.074
This pre-norm shape is the structure of every decoder block.

00:05:10.274 --> 00:05:15.545
A last RMSNorm at the output cleans that same stream before scoring.

00:05:15.745 --> 00:05:21.008
A separate, untied table of the same kind runs at the output, as you will see.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.054
A single vector knows its own meaning and nothing about its neighbors.

00:05:26.254 --> 00:05:30.264
The next problem is context: how a token learns what surrounds it.

00:05:30.464 --> 00:05:37.140
Self-attention solves this in one layer, by letting every token look back at the tokens before it.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:43.097
Each vector is projected three times: into a query, a key, and a value.

00:05:43.297 --> 00:05:45.447
The query is what a token asks for.

00:05:45.647 --> 00:05:48.593
The key is what a token offers for matching.

00:05:48.793 --> 00:05:53.925
The value is the information a token hands over once it is chosen.

00:05:54.125 --> 00:05:59.224
All three projections are learned matrices, multiplied into the stream.

00:05:59.424 --> 00:06:03.134
A query takes a dot product with every key in the sequence.

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:07.948
A large dot product means similar direction, long length, or both.

00:06:08.148 --> 00:06:11.452
Each dot product fills one cell of the score matrix.

00:06:11.652 --> 00:06:15.037
Those scores are divided by the square root of the key dimension.

00:06:15.237 --> 00:06:19.789
Without scaling, scores grow with the dimension and the softmax saturates.

00:06:19.989 --> 00:06:24.625
Scaling controls that growth and keeps the softmax away from saturation.

00:06:24.825 --> 00:06:28.680
The matrix on screen is the map of who will listen to whom.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.912
Scores are still raw numbers; they need to become weights.

00:06:33.112 --> 00:06:36.571
A causal mask hides the future from every query.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:41.610
A token may attend to itself and to earlier tokens, never later ones.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:46.647
Training never allows a token to read the answer it is being asked to predict.

00:06:46.847 --> 00:06:52.035
Masked scores are pushed to negative infinity before the softmax runs.

00:06:52.235 --> 00:06:57.221
Softmax turns the remaining scores into positive weights that sum to one.

00:06:57.421 --> 00:07:00.454
Look at the row for the word sat on screen.

00:07:00.654 --> 00:07:03.957
It places zero point eight six of its weight on cat.

00:07:04.157 --> 00:07:06.559
The masked future cell holds zero.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:11.278
The weights then blend the value vectors into a single output.

00:07:11.478 --> 00:07:16.342
The output is a weighted average of past values, so it stays near their neighborhood.

00:07:16.542 --> 00:07:20.472
Each token leaves attention carrying a summary of its own past.

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:25.322
This whole computation runs eight times in parallel, one per head.

00:07:25.522 --> 00:07:31.570
Query projections are per head, but keys and values are shared by pairs of heads.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.829
The eight outputs are concatenated and multiplied by one output matrix.

00:07:37.029 --> 00:07:40.204
The result is written back into the residual stream.

00:07:40.933 --> 00:07:47.789
Attention as written cannot see word order: shuffle the tokens and the outputs shuffle to match.

00:07:47.989 --> 00:07:53.878
The scores only see vectors, and the vectors so far carry meaning without position.

00:07:54.078 --> 00:07:58.139
So the next piece adds position without touching the meaning.

00:07:58.339 --> 00:08:03.462
Rotary embeddings fix this by rotating query and key vectors according to position.

00:08:03.662 --> 00:08:08.967
Each vector is split into pairs of dimensions, sixty-four pairs per head.

00:08:09.167 --> 00:08:14.223
Each pair is read as a point in two dimensions, or as a complex number.

00:08:14.423 --> 00:08:20.746
Position m rotates every pair by an angle of m times a base frequency.

00:08:20.946 --> 00:08:25.003
The rotation matrix on screen is the pair version of that turn.

00:08:25.203 --> 00:08:27.231
Different pairs turn at different speeds.

00:08:27.431 --> 00:08:33.113
Fast pairs pin down nearby structure; slow pairs mark long-range position.

00:08:33.313 --> 00:08:37.926
The fastest pair shown completes a full turn in about six point three tokens.

00:08:38.126 --> 00:08:44.540
When a rotated query meets a rotated key, their rotations combine into one relative turn.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:49.004
So the positional part of their dot product depends on the distance between them.

00:08:49.204 --> 00:08:54.925
Content still shapes the score; position adds only how far apart the two tokens sit.

00:08:55.125 --> 00:09:01.660
With theta at five hundred thousand, the slowest pairs stay smooth across two thousand forty-eight tokens.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:06.460
Theta sets the slowest wavelength; a larger theta pushes it further.

00:09:07.166 --> 00:09:10.990
One Lite detail lands between projection and rotation.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.650
Queries and keys each pass through RMSNorm before RoPE is applied.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:21.170
This controls the scale of each query and key, so the logits stay well behaved.

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:25.729
The norm runs per head, on each query and key vector on its own.

00:09:25.929 --> 00:09:32.699
Each layer has one shared gain of one hundred twenty-eight numbers for queries, and one for keys.

00:09:32.899 --> 00:09:38.197
The point of the step is training stability, at a tiny parameter cost.

00:09:38.397 --> 00:09:43.741
LLaMA 3 itself has no QK-norm; the step is a Lite addition.

00:09:44.466 --> 00:09:48.248
Attention during training sees the whole sentence at once.

00:09:48.448 --> 00:09:53.202
Generation is different: the model writes one token, then the next, then the next.

00:09:53.402 --> 00:09:56.587
Each new token still has to see everything before it.

00:09:56.787 --> 00:10:03.074
Recomputing every past key and value at every step would waste work.

00:10:03.274 --> 00:10:08.248
An inference-time optimization can store those vectors in a KV cache.

00:10:08.448 --> 00:10:12.621
Each layer stores two vectors per token for every key-value head.

00:10:12.821 --> 00:10:21.018
With sixteen layers and four key-value heads, one token costs sixteen thousand three hundred eighty-four stored numbers.

00:10:21.218 --> 00:10:24.744
That count assumes one hundred twenty-eight numbers per head.

00:10:24.944 --> 00:10:31.433
At two thousand forty-eight tokens of context, the cache passes thirty-three million numbers.

00:10:31.633 --> 00:10:35.087
The cache is a growing memory cost at inference time.

00:10:35.287 --> 00:10:40.545
LLaMA-3-Lite runs eight query heads and four key-value heads.

00:10:40.745 --> 00:10:44.623
Each key-value head is shared by two query heads.

00:10:44.823 --> 00:10:48.269
This layout is grouped-query attention.

00:10:48.469 --> 00:10:54.175
Four key-value heads halve the cache against eight, while the math of attention stays the same.

00:10:54.375 --> 00:11:00.573
The attention operation is unchanged, though a smaller key-value set can still change model quality.

00:11:01.266 --> 00:11:05.721
Two sublayers sit in every block; attention is the first.

00:11:05.921 --> 00:11:11.092
The second is a feed-forward network applied to each token on its own.

00:11:11.292 --> 00:11:17.218
Attention mixes tokens; the feed-forward works on each vector in isolation.

00:11:17.418 --> 00:11:24.228
It widens the vector from one thousand twenty-four to four thousand ninety-six, then compresses it back.

00:11:24.428 --> 00:11:29.163
The wide middle gives the block room to transform features between the two widths.

00:11:29.363 --> 00:11:36.734
Count the parameters and the feed-forward dominates: over twelve million per block against about three million for attention.

00:11:36.934 --> 00:11:39.890
LLaMA gates this expansion with SwiGLU.

00:11:40.090 --> 00:11:45.890
The wide layer splits into two parallel paths: a gate path and an up path.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.787
Both paths start from the same input vector.

00:11:48.987 --> 00:11:55.116
The gate path passes through the SiLU activation, which is x times its own sigmoid.

00:11:55.316 --> 00:12:02.178
Near zero, SiLU passes about half the input; large positive inputs pass nearly unchanged.

00:12:02.378 --> 00:12:05.985
Element by element, the gate path scales the up path.

00:12:06.185 --> 00:12:10.434
The gate can shrink a feature, pass it unchanged, or amplify it.

00:12:10.634 --> 00:12:13.642
Only the product of the two paths flows onward.

00:12:13.842 --> 00:12:19.288
Multiplicative gating lets one path decide how much of the other to keep.

00:12:19.488 --> 00:12:24.037
A final matrix projects the result back into the residual stream.

00:12:24.833 --> 00:12:29.046
After sixteen blocks, one last RMSNorm settles the stream.

00:12:29.246 --> 00:12:37.456
A separate, untied matrix scores all one hundred twenty-eight thousand two hundred fifty-six candidates.

00:12:37.656 --> 00:12:43.894
Each score, called a logit, is one dot product between the final vector and one candidate row.

00:12:44.094 --> 00:12:52.882
This unembedding table is as large as the embedding table: about one hundred thirty-one million parameters in each.

00:12:53.082 --> 00:12:56.959
Together, the two tables hold just over half of the model.

00:12:57.159 --> 00:13:01.146
Large logits mean the candidate fits the context well.

00:13:01.346 --> 00:13:06.465
A softmax turns the logits into probabilities over the whole vocabulary.

00:13:06.665 --> 00:13:11.700
Sampling picks one token, and that token is appended to the context.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:15.547
The full network then runs again for the next one.

00:13:15.747 --> 00:13:19.961
One forward pass per generated token; that is the generation loop.

00:13:20.161 --> 00:13:26.563
A KV cache would keep past keys and values, so each new pass computes only the newest token.

00:13:26.763 --> 00:13:33.214
Without one, every step re-reads the whole sequence; this code does exactly that.

00:13:33.414 --> 00:13:38.517
What began as one function on a table of numbers now writes a paragraph.

00:13:39.233 --> 00:13:43.576
Walk the whole path once more, from text to a sampled token.

00:13:43.776 --> 00:13:48.591
The tokenizer cuts the sentence into six pieces with integer IDs.

00:13:48.791 --> 00:13:54.839
The embedding table turns each ID into a vector of one thousand twenty-four numbers.

00:13:55.039 --> 00:13:59.146
The residual stream carries those vectors through sixteen blocks.

00:13:59.346 --> 00:14:08.700
In each block, RMSNorm controls scale, attention mixes context, and the gated feed-forward rewrites features.

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:13.572
Rotary embeddings keep the order of the tokens visible through all of it.

00:14:13.772 --> 00:14:21.048
Grouped-query attention keeps the cache affordable; QK-norm keeps the logits stable.

00:14:21.248 --> 00:14:25.664
The unembedding table scores every candidate, and sampling takes one.

00:14:25.864 --> 00:14:33.082
About five hundred fifteen million parameters, and one function from tokens to probabilities.
